 "narrative": [
  "## What the business is and where cash sits",
  "Paper in Muzaffarnagar is mostly converting: waste paper and pulp in, kraft/board/paper plates out, sold to local traders and FMCG packers. **Cash sits in inventory and receivables, not in the machine.** Peer rating work shows the pattern: suppliers paid in advance while customers take 45-60 days to pay, forcing reliance on working-capital bank lines [8]. So when a proposal lands, check the receivable days and advance-to-supplier terms first; if receivables exceed 60 days, ask for the ageing and the top-five buyer list.",
  "## What has changed now",
  "Two things. First, local labour: a paper-plate factory in Titawi was raided over 12 bonded labourers, with an SIT formed and an NHRC notice to the state [2][3]; a worker at a Parijaat paper mill on Bhopa Road died in suspicious circumstances [1]. **Labour compliance is now a credit event, not an HR issue.** Second, sector economics: ICRA flags volatile realisations from cheaper imports and high input and energy costs [11], and water-treatment lapses can bring penalties and stoppage [10]. Peer results are mixed: Kuantum's Q4FY26 PAT rose 46% QoQ to INR14 crores on 22% EBITDA growth [6], while Pudumjee's FY26 net profit slipped to INR94 crore from INR96 crore [5].",
  "## Where to grow",
  "UP GST collections are running +14.2% Apr-to-date against India's +6.1% [D], so the local demand pull is real. **Pursue converters and paper-plate/board units selling into UP's packaging and food-service chain, and second-generation promoters with owned premises.** Avoid pulp-integrated mills and any unit with pending labour or pollution proceedings. India-wide IIP for paper (NIC 17) is -0.1% YoY [D], so do not underwrite volume growth from the sector; underwrite the borrower's order book.",
  "## How to grow safely",
  "Structure as working-capital-led: cash credit against stock and receivables, term loan only for proven debottlenecking. **Take monthly stock-and-receivable statements, quarterly GST returns, and a labour-compliance declaration every quarter.** Covenant on receivable days and on director guarantees. For new units, start at 60% of assessed drawing power and raise after two clean quarters.",
  "## Early-warning signs",
  "Watch for stretching creditor days, sudden buyer concentration, and any labour-inspection notice. **Any NHRC or labour-department notice is an immediate stop-and-review trigger.** Also watch power and water-treatment penalty notices [10]."
 ],
